00:56:38.620 --> 00:56:39.190
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Yeah.

425
00:56:39.420 --> 00:56:55.170
Samuel Smith: Yeah, that's not… that's not technically a signature, it's an integrity proof. That's why you can… you can verify it, because it's using HMACs under the hood. So, yeah, it's… it's… it's problematic, right? The integrity proofs are problematic from that respect, yes.

426
00:56:55.170 --> 00:57:04.469
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): So, so one of the things that I worried about is what happens if you embed a W3C verifiable credential, and the embedded data

