428
00:57:10.530 --> 00:57:15.550
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): you know, some kind of a JSON transformation happens that's not substantive.

429
00:57:15.740 --> 00:57:16.400
Samuel Smith: Yep.

430
00:57:18.390 --> 00:57:22.330
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): You could end up in a situation where

431
00:57:22.360 --> 00:57:41.809
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): you have embedded something that doesn't look like the same thing anymore, and yet the intention was that it be the same thing, you know? I don't know exactly how to deal with that. If you say, look, we will simply embed the W3C credential as a sequence of bytes, and we will hash it.

432
00:57:42.130 --> 00:57:42.960
Samuel Smith: Yep.

433
00:57:42.960 --> 00:57:48.889
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): You get, perfect fidelity, but you get,

434
00:57:49.770 --> 00:57:56.899
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): you have to make sure that it's treated as a sequence of bytes rather than as JSON at every place where

435
00:57:57.020 --> 00:57:59.559
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): It might be processed.

436
00:58:00.380 --> 00:58:15.659
Samuel Smith: Yeah, so… so… so the… the… so there's really two ways to look at this problem. One is, are you treating the ACDC as purely a container? If you're treating it as purely a container, then the only one that makes sense is that you're going to hash

437
00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:17.260
Samuel Smith: some…

438
00:58:17.570 --> 00:58:26.010
Samuel Smith: Some digital expression, which means that's fixed, you can't change it, and you're gonna… and you're going to put the hash

439
00:58:26.200 --> 00:58:35.029
Samuel Smith: as the value of the cargo field, and say, I'm making a commitment to this exact serialization. There's no semantic.

440
00:58:35.460 --> 00:58:45.339
Samuel Smith: there's no semantic equivalents involved. The other interpretation is, I want the ACDC to convey some semantic

441
00:58:45.340 --> 00:59:01.429
Samuel Smith: values that is embedded in the W3C verifiable credential, and that opens up a can of worms. Not that that can of worms can't be resolved, but it does open up a can of worms. And so, I think in some cases, the simple thing is, well.

442
00:59:01.430 --> 00:59:05.709
Samuel Smith: we're gonna treat the ACDC as a container, it's not conveying any semantics at all.

443
00:59:05.780 --> 00:59:18.809
Samuel Smith: And it's up to the user to… you're basically layering the protocol. You're saying, the ACDC is a layer that says, I'm going to transport something. Once I transport it, you can verify that it wasn't tampered with.

444
00:59:18.890 --> 00:59:36.899
Samuel Smith: And then, if you want to do equivalences that say this is semantically equivalent, which is what the integrity proof says, I can have two different RDF graphs, they're semantically equivalent, and the same integrity proof will verify against both of them, so I know that I didn't lose anything semantically, even though

445
00:59:36.950 --> 00:59:46.279
Samuel Smith: Both of them were conveyed differently in their different serializations, and… but that meant they were conveyed in two separate cases across

446
00:59:46.510 --> 01:00:02.640
Samuel Smith: two separate ACDCs that conveyed them, so they're not the same conveyance, but at an upper layer, you can process. So I'm worried about the layer inversion of semantics and treating ACDCs as containers.

447
01:00:02.720 --> 01:00:10.769
Samuel Smith: ACDCs are also not containers when we use them directly as credentials, so there's semantics embedded in it, and that's, you know, that's…

448
01:00:11.390 --> 01:00:25.100
Samuel Smith: That's something we have to be careful about, right? We have to say, well, we're using this ACDC semantically as not just a container, but as something that the fields have meaning, they have semantics.

449
01:00:25.100 --> 01:00:49.670
Samuel Smith: And when you… but when we want to cross the bridge to W3C, there's a bunch of semantic baggage that comes with that W3C credential, and I don't mean baggage in a bad way, I just mean there's, you know, it's like somebody showing up at the cruise and going, oh, you know, I've got a truckload of bags that need to get on board the ship. You know, it's not just what I'm carrying on my purse.

450
01:00:50.300 --> 01:00:58.789
Samuel Smith: So… so… so I… so yes, there is… there is a danger there, and I'm wondering if we should make a clean separation and say, hey.

451
01:00:59.750 --> 01:01:03.899
Samuel Smith: If you're just going to use ACDC as a container.

452
01:01:04.280 --> 01:01:21.989
Samuel Smith: then just hash whatever it is that you're transporting, put the hash in the container, and you're done. Anything else is happening outside of the purview of the ACDC. If you want to… if you want to impose semantics, then in each case, you have to define what those semantics are.

453
01:01:23.810 --> 01:01:29.530
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Yeah, I see the attractiveness of the, of the simplicity of simply saying, just hash everything.

454
01:01:30.120 --> 01:01:40.090
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): I, I think… that might be where we have to start. And maybe we actually say.

455
01:01:40.450 --> 01:01:44.679
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): you know, the ACDC is a container, and everything's hashed.

456
01:01:44.910 --> 01:02:04.499
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): And if you want additional intelligence, you have to introduce another layer, which says, I'm going to look at the MIME type of the thing that I hashed, and, you know, if I can tell that the MIME type is a verifiable credential, then I will impose some additional logic, but it's not something that the ACDC layer has to know anything about. That might be better.

457
01:02:06.280 --> 01:02:09.080
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Okay.

458
01:02:09.080 --> 01:02:13.009
Samuel Smith: And we… and we have a… we actually have an attachment group

459
01:02:13.490 --> 01:02:18.569
Samuel Smith: Now, we added in the version 2 of the spec where you can, attach

460
01:02:18.850 --> 01:02:21.540
Samuel Smith: A blindable, mind type.

461
01:02:22.080 --> 01:02:31.040
Samuel Smith: And a… and a hash of… of the actual document, so that… so that,

462
01:02:31.380 --> 01:02:37.829
Samuel Smith: you know, you can do it in a blinded fashion, so it's a blinded hash.

463
01:02:37.940 --> 01:02:43.339
Samuel Smith: So you just put that in the cargo field, and then you can attach whatever you… then you attach that

464
01:02:43.530 --> 01:02:48.969
Samuel Smith: That attachment, and then that attachment makes a cryptographic commitment

465
01:02:49.090 --> 01:02:53.209
Samuel Smith: To some other thing which… which has a mine type.

466
01:02:53.580 --> 01:03:09.829
Samuel Smith: And then… and then… and then however you convey that other thing, whether it's attached as well or comes through a separate channel, doesn't matter. The commitment is all verifiable at the end, and it's blindable because… because you're… you're putting a blinding factor in the hash computation.

467
01:03:10.320 --> 01:03:24.250
Samuel Smith: Which… which is… which is left out of what you're proposing, and I think that's a valuable thing to always do when you're doing hashes of things that are… that have information that might be sensitive.

468
01:03:24.460 --> 01:03:39.140
Samuel Smith: you… you want to have the hashes be blinded so that the only way that somebody can verify the hash is for… is to be presented with the un… to unblind it at the point of presentation, and so that at least they have some control over that.

469
01:03:39.470 --> 01:03:40.350
Samuel Smith: So that…

470
01:03:40.390 --> 01:03:52.460
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): So, so, I know we need to end, but let me just ask. So, all of these things that we're talking about here would be referenced in a dossier by, an Edge.

