Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): you want a second layer of disclosability, which is when you get to that ACDC, the ACDC says, here's the content that I'm committed to, but I'm not going to tell you what its MIME type is, or anything else about it, I'm just going to tell you its… its content hash.

476
01:04:24.130 --> 01:04:31.350
Samuel Smith: Yeah, and that's obviously optional, but what happens is when you look at, things like…

477
01:04:31.480 --> 01:04:35.689
Samuel Smith: Reducing vendor-to-vendor correlation.

478
01:04:36.010 --> 01:04:45.910
Samuel Smith: in order to verify the ACDC, if the edges require you to, to, to verify the edge.

479
01:04:46.200 --> 01:04:50.730
Samuel Smith: Then… then you… then you have to… then you have to unblind the edge hash.

480
01:04:50.880 --> 01:05:06.779
Samuel Smith: But you don't have to unblind the content of the container to do that verification. So having that extra indirection means that you still can keep the content of the container private, even though you've correlated the container itself.

481
01:05:07.010 --> 01:05:09.430
Samuel Smith: Because… Yeah. So…

482
01:05:09.430 --> 01:05:10.140
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Get it.

483
01:05:10.740 --> 01:05:14.500
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Yeah, you would… you would have… Yeah.

484
01:05:18.080 --> 01:05:18.980
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Okay.

485
01:05:20.090 --> 01:05:24.079
Samuel Smith: So it's all about… it's all about partitioning the context.

486
01:05:24.300 --> 01:05:27.139
Samuel Smith: So that you have, a need-to-know

487
01:05:27.530 --> 01:05:34.810
Samuel Smith: contextual partitioning, so you have this metadata that's just floating around, and when you look at it, you go, well, who needs to verify X?

488
01:05:34.870 --> 01:05:49.520
Samuel Smith: do they need to also verify Y? That means if… if there's a difference between X and Y, then you would have a different blind… blinding factor, you know, you have another layer of blinding, because that's… that's how you…

489
01:05:49.520 --> 01:06:04.480
Samuel Smith: That's how you manage that. I mean, and that's when I did the SPAC protocol, that became obvious that the way that you manage privacy without sacrificing security is that you have layered contexts

490
01:06:04.700 --> 01:06:05.790
Samuel Smith: where…

491
01:06:05.990 --> 01:06:16.319
Samuel Smith: Where you can so, you know, compartmentalize what gets… what is correlatable, and what is disclosed, and if you get, you know.

492
01:06:16.570 --> 01:06:20.759
Samuel Smith: Enough layers to cover the end-to-end

493
01:06:20.870 --> 01:06:35.249
Samuel Smith: transport, then… then that's all you need, and… and… and so sometimes you just need one more layer, right? And then… and then… and then you can cover… cover the… the full… the full use case without… and still do that. And sometimes.

494
01:06:35.250 --> 01:06:43.699
